 This set has n*n=n^2 elements, so it cannot contain n^2+1 distinct ordered pairs. This contradiction shows that the assumption was false. Hence the original sequence contains a strictly increasing subsequence of length n+1 or a strictly decreasing subsequence of length n+1.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "rank_pair_injection", "attempt_type": "direct", "subgoal": "Complete target theorem", "attempt_summary": "Assuming both monotone lengths are at most n puts n^2+1 distinct rank pairs in an n^2-element grid.", "status": "solved", "used_examples": [], "used_counterexamples": [], "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "summary": "All subgoals solved.", "result": "not_needed"}, "key_stuck_points": [], "used_results": [], "adapted_from": [], "migration_failures": [], "branch_id": "rank_pair_injection"}, "glossary": {"n": "The positive integer in the theorem.", "x_i": "The i-th term of the given sequence.", "I_i": "The maximum length of a strictly increasing subsequence ending at x_i.", "D_i": "The maximum length of a strictly decreasing subsequence ending at x_i.", "i": "A position index in the sequence.", "j": "A position index later than i."}}
